course: biology
lesson: 246
title: Defences: venom in snakes, jellyfish, scorpions and the platypus
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Venom is a poison that an animal pushes into another animal with fangs, a sting or a spine, like a tiny syringe.
explain: There is an easy rule: if it bites you and you get hurt, it is venomous; if you bite it and you get hurt, it is poisonous.
explain: Venom is mostly for catching food fast, and for defence: a slow cone snail must stop a fast fish at once, and a box jellyfish must stop a fish before it tears its delicate tentacles.
explain: Many venomous animals warn before they strike, the way a cobra spreads its hood, because a fight is risky and venom is costly to make.
explain: So if you meet a snake on a path, the best thing is the simplest: stay calm, back away slowly and let it go.
fact: Venom is injected by a bite, a sting or a spine, while a poison is in the body and harms you if you eat or touch it; a few animals, like the slow loris, are both.
fact: Venomous snakes use venom mainly to catch and kill prey and to defend themselves, and only about one in seven kinds of snake is dangerous to people.
fact: Box jellyfish have tentacles covered in tiny stinging cells that fire like harpoons, and lifeguards in Australia pour vinegar on the stings to stop the unfired cells.
fact: A cobra spreads its hood by stretching long ribs in its neck as a warning, and some cobras spit venom at an attacker's eyes from about two metres away.
fact: Male platypuses have venom spurs on their back legs for fights with other males; the venom can cause terrible pain for weeks but has never been known to kill a person, and the platypus is one of very few venomous mammals.
fact: Cone snails shoot a harpoon-like tooth full of venom into fish, and pain medicines have been made from cone snail venom.
fact: A scorpion has eight legs, like its cousin the spider, plus two big claws at the front and a sting on its tail.
fact: Scorpions glow blue-green under ultraviolet light, nobody is quite sure why, and only a few of the many kinds are dangerous to people.
fact: A tarantula bite is usually no worse than a bee sting to a person, and many American tarantulas defend themselves by flicking tiny itchy hairs from their bottoms.
fact: Antivenom is made by giving horses small doses of venom and collecting the antibodies their bodies make; snakebites still kill tens of thousands of people a year, mostly farmers in poor countries.
fact: Komodo dragons, the biggest lizards alive at up to 3 metres, have venom glands in their jaws; people long thought they killed with germs in dirty spit, until venom was reported in 2009.
fact: Snakes cannot hypnotise: they have no eyelids, so they seem to stare, and a charmer's cobra follows the moving pipe, not the music, because snakes hear sounds in the air poorly and feel vibrations through their jaws.
fact: If a snake is on a path, stand still or back away slowly, because snakes bite mostly when trapped or stepped on; if someone is bitten, keep them still and get help fast, and never cut the bite or suck out venom.
fact: In Britain the only venomous snake is the adder, and bites are rare.
exercise: What is the difference between venomous and poisonous? | Venom is injected, poison harms you when eaten or touched | They mean exactly the same thing, so either word is right | Venomous animals are always bigger than poisonous animals | Poisonous animals always bite
exercise: A snake bites you and it hurts. Is the snake venomous or poisonous? | Venomous | Poisonous | Both, always | Neither
exercise: What do venomous snakes mainly use venom for? | Catching prey and defending themselves | Digesting the tough leaves they eat | Keeping warm in winter | Making their skin shiny and smooth so they slide along
exercise: About how many kinds of snake are dangerous to people? | About one in seven | Every single kind | About nine in ten | None at all
exercise: How does a box jellyfish sting? | Tiny stinging cells on its tentacles fire like harpoons | It bites with rows of tiny sharp teeth | It squirts a jet of burning acid out of the top of its bell | It wraps round and squeezes
exercise: What do lifeguards in Australia pour on box jellyfish stings? | Vinegar | Hot water | Sea water mixed with sand | Fizzy drink
exercise: Why does a cobra spread its hood? | To warn an attacker to stay away | To catch the wind and glide | To keep cool in the sun | To catch insects in it
exercise: What is special about a male platypus's back legs? | They have venom spurs | They have webbed claws for digging only | They have hooves | They glow in the dark
exercise: Why does a slow cone snail need fast venom? | It must stop a fast fish at once | It must chase fish across the reef | It uses venom to build its shell | It uses venom to hide
exercise: What do scorpions do under ultraviolet light? | Glow blue-green | Turn invisible | Change into red | Go to sleep at once
exercise: How bad is a tarantula bite for most people? | Usually no worse than a bee sting | Always deadly within a few minutes | Worse than any snake bite in the world | It cannot bite at all
exercise: How is antivenom made? | Horses get small doses of venom and their antibodies are collected | Snakes are milked and the venom is boiled until it is safe | It is made from sugar and salt and a little lemon juice, mixed well | It is squeezed from plants
exercise: What did scientists report about Komodo dragons in 2009? | They have venom glands in their jaws | They breathe fire like dragons | They are poisonous to eat | They have no teeth in their mouths at all
exercise: Why do snakes seem to stare without blinking? | They have no eyelids | They are trying to hypnotise you | They are always asleep | They cannot move their heads
exercise: What does a snake charmer's cobra follow? | The moving pipe | The music | The smell of the charmer | The light of the sun
exercise: You see a snake on the path ahead. What should you do? | Stay calm, back away slowly and let it go | Run straight at it and shout to scare it off | Pick it up by the tail and throw it | Poke it with a stick
exercise: Which is the only venomous snake in Britain? | The adder | The cobra | The grass snake | The python
test: In The Jungle Book, Kaa the python hypnotises Mowgli with spiral eyes. What is true? | Snakes cannot hypnotise, they just cannot blink | Pythons really hypnotise their prey with their stare | Only very old snakes can hypnotise | Snakes hypnotise with their tongues
test: «mate got bit by a snake on the trail, should i suck the venom out like in the films» | No, keep him still and get help fast | Yes, as fast as you can | Yes, after cutting the bite open | No, make him run to the car
test: «platypus is a cute duck-beaver, can't hurt anyone lol» What is right? | A male's back-leg spurs carry venom that causes terrible pain | It has a venomous bite with fangs, like a cobra | It is completely harmless in every way | It spits a stream of poison from its beak at anyone who comes close to its burrow
test: «2 scorpions in the tank. how many legs, not counting the claws» | 16 | 12 | 20 | 8
test: «3 cobras and 1 adder. tails, how many» | 4 | 3 | 8 | 12
test: «my brother says a tarantula will kill u in a minute, that's why he won't hold it» What is true? | A bite is usually no worse than a bee sting, though their itchy hairs can annoy | Yes, every tarantula can kill a grown-up in about a minute with one bite | Tarantulas have no fangs at all | Only baby tarantulas are dangerous
test: What would happen if there were no antivenom? | Many more people bitten by snakes would die | Snakes would stop being venomous | Horses would become venomous | Nothing would change
test: A cobra spreads its hood and hisses at a dog. Why warn instead of biting at once? | A fight is risky and venom is costly to make | Cobras have no venom at all | It is asking the dog to play a game of chase | It wants the dog to follow it
test: «komodo dragons kill u with dirty germ spit, everyone knows that» What is more accurate? | They have venom glands in their jaws, found in 2009 | Yes, only germs and no venom | They kill only by breathing fire | They are plant eaters
test: You bite a pufferfish dish and get ill. Was the fish venomous or poisonous? | Poisonous | Venomous | Neither, fish cannot harm you | Both, always
test: Why might doctors study cone snail venom? | Pain medicines have been made from it | It makes shells grow on people | It cures colds instantly after one drop | It turns water into fuel for cars and boats
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the distinction between venomous and poisonous organisms? | Venom is injected by bite, sting or spine, whereas poison causes harm when ingested or touched | The terms are wholly synonymous in scientific usage, both denoting any harmful substance | Poisonous organisms invariably inflict bites | Venomous organisms are harmful solely when consumed or touched by the predator
exam: Identify the principal method by which antivenom is produced. | The administration of small doses of venom to horses and the collection of the resulting antibodies | The boiling of venom extracted from snakes until all of its harmful properties are destroyed entirely | The extraction of juices from tropical plants | The dilution of venom in sea water
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the conduct of a person who encounters a snake upon a footpath. | The person should remain calm, retreat slowly and permit the snake to depart | The person should seize the snake firmly by the tail and remove it from the path | The person should strike the snake repeatedly with a stick | The person should run directly towards the snake
exam: Ascertain the purpose served by the spreading of the hood in the cobra. | The issuing of a warning to a potential attacker | The capture of airborne insects | The regulation of body temperature in the sun | The attraction of prey by imitation of a flower
